{
 "question": "Where does the AC-3 decoder spend the time of a frame, and what of it is spent on nothing: work whose result it already has, or that nobody reads?",
 "method": "Timers were put around the stages of the decoder in a throwaway build and `verify` run over one stream of each kind; the shares are in `where_the_time_of_a_frame_went`. Three things stood out that change no sample. The bit allocation is fixed-point arithmetic of exponents and parameters, and it was computed for every channel in every block though four in five had the inputs of the one before: it is kept and written again. The record of a block, some twenty vectors, was made for every block and read by two inspection commands only: it is made where it is asked for. And a block decoded its coefficients into seven arrays and copied out the channels it had: it decodes where it hands them out. Each change is a commit and was measured against the one before it with `tools/bench.py --compare`, two binaries turn and turn about, five runs a command; then the three together against main, and beside FFmpeg (`--refs`). That no sample and no report changed was looked for in the reports and deliveries of both binaries over the corpus (`tools/old_new.py --decode-all`), in 320 damaged copies of the fixture, in the campaign of evidence 22 on main and on the change, and in what the two commands that read the record print and write. The streams of the corpus are media and are not in the repository.",
 "machine": {
